li><li>Tornadoes</li><li>Frozen pipes</li><li>Tree damage</li></ul></div>

<h2>Claims in a city with every kind of house</h2>
<p>Since the city and Marion County merged under Unigov in 1970, Indianapolis has covered almost the whole county, which means an appraiser working here sees nearly every type of Indiana construction. Lockerbie Square and the Old Northside hold 19th-century brick homes with slate and tin details. Irvington, Meridian-Kessler and Butler-Tarkington are full of 1910s–1930s bungalows and Foursquares with plaster walls, original millwork and clay-tile or asbestos-era roofing layers underneath. Lawrence, Warren and Perry townships are dominated by post-war ranches and split-levels, while Franklin and Decatur townships keep adding newer subdivisions.</p>
<p>Weather here is a steady diet of spring and summer thunderstorms. Hail swaths regularly cross the metro from the west, straight-line winds topple mature trees along the White River and Fall Creek corridors, and January cold snaps freeze pipes in older homes with uninsulated exterior walls.</p>
<h2>Disputes we see most in Indianapolis</h2>
<ul>
<li>Plaster-and-lath walls priced as standard half-inch drywall.</li>
<li>Historic preservation commission requirements left out of the repair scope in protected districts.</li>
<li>Hail claims on older roofs denied as prior damage or wear.</li>
<li>Tree removal limits applied to large-canopy removals on tight lots.</li>
<li>Condo water losses where the master policy and unit owner policy point at each other.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Neighborhoods we cover</h2>
<p>Broad Ripple, Fountain Square, Irvington, Meridian-Kessler, Butler-Tarkington, Herron-Morton, Old Northside, Lockerbie Square, Castleton, Nora, Speedway, Beech Grove, Lawrence, Warren, Perry, Franklin, Decatur, Pike and Wayne townships, plus surrounding communities in Hamilton, Hendricks, Johnson and Hancock counties.</p>

